{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-39161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+6\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-39161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1-6\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-39161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64ecbc01a567ba70c8a8880ffa9545bffc243e13a75622951c88566d647a54c4"} {"question" "q-39161" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-39161" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-39161" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-39166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-39166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +52 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=4\\pm 6\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4-6\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=4-6\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4-6\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+6\\text{i} & -3 \\\\ 15 & -3+6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(3+6\\text{i}){x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 15{x}_{1}+(-3+6\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-39166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64ecbc01a567ba70c8a8880ffa9545bffc243e13a75622951c88566d647a54c4"} {"question" "q-39166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-39166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{15}{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-39166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-99342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-99342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-99342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64ecbc01a567ba70c8a8880ffa9545bffc243e13a75622951c88566d647a54c4"} {"question" "q-99342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-99342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-99342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-140498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-1 \\\\ \\text{i}+2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-140498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-140498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64ecbc01a567ba70c8a8880ffa9545bffc243e13a75622951c88566d647a54c4"} {"question" "q-140498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-140498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-140498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-140501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-140501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+2\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-1+2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 2 & 2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(2-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-140501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "64ecbc01a567ba70c8a8880ffa9545bffc243e13a75622951c88566d647a54c4"} {"question" "q-140501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-140501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-140501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/state.json" "file_sha256" "ed70bdad52458052316810f9e5bdb87e45fc3e5867b140b1a084d58752275dd1"} {"question" "q-39161" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "977ff64391a33defa3c3c07c384f9a7b9978c75af7191e39f4f7fd28e3fddf24" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "The authentic solution constructs P from the real and imaginary parts of conjugate(v)=(i,2-3i), giving columns (0,2) and (1,-3). Its determinant is -2, and these columns give AP=PC for C=[[1,-6],[6,1]], hence A=PCP^-1. This proves original option b (index 1), matching saved Correct grading. The stored Q=[[-1,0],[3,2]] is also valid: Q=PR with R=[[0,1],[-1,0]], and R commutes with C, so QCQ^-1=PCP^-1. Thus Q and P are unequal matrices that yield the same A; a normalizer must not equate their entries. This review documents the source-backed representative without declaring the alternative mathematically wrong." "bindings" {"problem" "1b56f67adadedfd98ba955574a7a7641e5d5a4c0a534e77a3a8ac42931f74135" "worked_solution" "a04b7750abfda440f9726a2dae498313d8077dc5aad11d39cfddb57a244960bd" "original_field" "57e42df675c52f352a5386d7b5d86997ca81b13035e544052f55f7fb9d8e4e62" "reviewed_field" "5ad9fc5d0808bc79df2a32760d36fb9b77f157fd60551168121f0d6cdfbf471d" "raw_widget" "ebbb1dbb9ab42355006aca9633301cd61ba176e97e7ec6ab6fbea0bd3730cacd"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-39161-before.png" "sha256" "2d72b11a0809855c3c02335bacf237babb9ecd0598216ea8394318a5c9f494e2"} {"path" "q-39161-before.json" "sha256" "3eaeef8d8f3cd6f113a7a496841c03b23d56edc882d83543447b10eb404dc3f2"} {"path" "history-q-39161.png" "sha256" "c156eb637f4b6cf9cf6bbec7c50998e3d844c6e99e5e6b8d2823d597a25f0a2d"} {"path" "q-39161-after.json" "sha256" "9660edd030b6c436ff28da87b065e460e10d27cabd45047dd6b76ede624a2ae1"}]} {"question" "q-39161" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075172/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "977ff64391a33defa3c3c07c384f9a7b9978c75af7191e39f4f7fd28e3fddf24" "rationale" "The authentic solution constructs P from the real and imaginary parts of conjugate(v)=(i,2-3i), giving columns (0,2) and (1,-3). Its determinant is -2, and these columns give AP=PC for C=[[1,-6],[6,1]], hence A=PCP^-1. This proves original option b (index 1), matching saved Correct grading. The stored Q=[[-1,0],[3,2]] is also valid: Q=PR with R=[[0,1],[-1,0]], and R commutes with C, so QCQ^-1=PCP^-1. Thus Q and P are unequal matrices that yield the same A; a normalizer must not equate their entries. This review documents the source-backed representative without declaring the alternative mathematically wrong."}] "usage" {"q-39161" []} "basis" 2755 "retractions" [[17592186129656 :question/problem "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 + 3\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 1 + 6\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A = PCP^{-1}$."] [17592186129656 :question/answer-fields 17592186127730]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127731 17592186127732 17592186127733 17592186127734 17592186127735] "immutable_fields" [[17592186127730 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127730 :answer-field/id] [17592186127730 :answer-field/key] [17592186127730 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
